  translation: "1. Arjuna said: What is that Brahman? What concerns the self? What is action, O supreme Person? And what is declared to concern beings? What is called what concerns the gods?\n2. How, and who, is the lord of sacrifice here in this body, O Madhusūdana? And how are you to be known at the time of departure by those of steady self?\n3. The blessed Lord said: The Imperishable is the supreme Brahman. One's own being is called what concerns the self. The offering that brings beings to birth is what is termed action.\n4. What concerns beings is perishable existence, and the Person is what concerns the deity. I myself am the lord of sacrifice here in the body, O best of embodied beings.\n5. And he who at the time of death departs remembering me alone, having left the body, goes to my state. There is no doubt of this.\n6. Whatever being a man remembers as he leaves the body at the end, to that very being he goes, O son of Kuntī, having always been shaped by that state.\n7. Therefore remember me at all times, and fight. With mind and intellect given over to me, you will come to me alone. Of this there is no doubt.\n8. With a mind joined to the yoga of practice and not going elsewhere, one goes, O Pārtha, meditating, to the supreme divine Person.\n9. One should call to mind the seer, the ancient, the ruler, finer even than an atom: the ordainer of all, whose form cannot be conceived, bright as the sun, beyond the darkness.\n10. At the time of departure, with an unmoving mind, joined with devotion and by the power of yoga, he who has rightly fixed the breath between the eyebrows attains that supreme divine Person.\n11. That Imperishable which the knowers of the Veda declare, which strivers from whom passion has gone enter, and desiring which they practice the student's discipline: that goal of yours I shall declare in brief.\n12. Having restrained all the gates and confined the mind in the heart, having placed one's own breath at the crown of the head, engaged in the concentration of yoga,\n13. Uttering Om, the one syllable, Brahman, and contemplating me, he who dies, leaving the body, goes to the highest goal.\n14. I am easy to reach, O Pārtha, for the yogin whose mind is on no other object and who remembers me unceasingly through life, his mind always collected.\n15. Having reached me, the great-souled do not come to rebirth, that unstable abode of sorrows. They have reached the highest perfection.\n16. The worlds, as far as the world of Brahmā, return again, O Arjuna. But having reached me alone, O son of Kuntī, there is no rebirth.\n17. They know that day of Brahmā which ends at a thousand yugas, and the night which ends at a thousand yugas. Those people know the measure of day and night.\n18. From the unmanifest all manifest beings come forth when the day comes. When the night comes they dissolve into that same unmanifest.\n19. This very same mass of beings, having come to be again and again, dissolves when the night comes, not of its own will, O Pārtha, and comes forth when the day comes.\n20. But other than that unmanifest is another being, itself unmanifest and ancient. When all beings perish, that one does not perish.\n21. That unmanifest, called the imperishable, they call the highest goal. Having reached it, they do not return. That highest place is mine.\n22. That supreme Person, O Pārtha, is reached by devotion that goes to no other, devotion whose object is the Self. Beings stand within him, and by him this whole world is pervaded.\n23. The time at which those who have departed go to non-return, and the time at which they return, I shall declare, O best of Bharatas. Yogins here are both the men of discipline and those who perform rites.\n24. Fire, light, day, the bright fortnight, the six months of the northern course: the people who depart by that path, knowers of Brahman, the worshippers of Brahman, go to Brahman.\n25. Smoke, night, the dark fortnight, the six months of the southern course: the yogin, the man of rites, reaches the lunar light there and returns.\n26. These are the two paths of the world, the bright and the dark, held to be eternal. By the one he goes to non-return; by the other he returns again.\n27. Knowing these two paths, O Pārtha, no yogin at all is confused. Therefore at all times be intent on yoga, O Arjuna.\n28. Whatever fruit of merit scripture appoints in the Vedas rightly studied, in sacrifices rightly offered, in austerities, and in gifts, the yogin goes beyond it all once he has understood this teaching and put it into practice, and he attains the highest place, the first, which is Brahman.",
